// hebrew-demo — right-to-left Hebrew typesetting in texish.
//
// Render with:  texish-cli scripts/hebrew-demo.script
//
// texish lays out and breaks every paragraph in logical (reading) order, exactly
// as Knuth TeX does. For Hebrew that is not enough: the characters must also be
// reordered into visual order for display. The engine now runs the Unicode
// Bidirectional Algorithm (UAX #9) over each set line — choosing the paragraph's
// base direction, resolving embedded numbers and Latin runs, and reversing the
// right-to-left runs — and hands every backend glyphs already in visual order, so
// the Cairo PDF, the JVM raster, the SVG and the in-browser canvas all agree.
//
// \rtl sets a right-to-left base (the paragraph flushes right and its last line
// rags left); \ltr restores the default. The bundled Noto Serif Hebrew face is
// selected with \font hebrew, in a regular and a bold cut. The text below is the
// opening of John from Franz Delitzsch's Hebrew New Testament (public domain),
// set unpointed — niqqud positioning is a later, separate addition.

\use{document}

\title{Right-to-Left Hebrew in \TeXish}
\author{The texish engine}
\maketitle

\section{Setting a Hebrew paragraph}

With a right-to-left base direction the engine reverses each run of Hebrew within
a line, reorders the words, and aligns the whole paragraph to the right margin.
The line breaker is untouched — it still finds its breaks at the interword glue,
in logical order — and only the set lines are turned into visual order. Below are
the first five verses of the Gospel of John, set as one flowing paragraph at
fourteen points so the right-to-left justification shows across several lines.

\medskip

\rtl
{\font hebrew 14 regular
בראשית היה הדבר והדבר היה את האלהים ואלהים היה הדבר׃ הוא היה בראשית אצל האלהים׃
הכל נהיה על־ידו ומבלעדיו לא נהיה כל־אשר נהיה׃ בו היו חיים והחיים היו אור בני האדם׃
והאור בחשך זרח והחשך לא השיגו׃
}
\ltr

\section{A bold cut}

The bundled face ships a real bold weight, so emphasis is set by the font rather
than faked. Here the opening words of the passage are set in bold Hebrew:

\medskip

\rtl
{\font hebrew 18 bold
בראשית היה הדבר׃
}
\ltr

\section{Pointed text}

Hebrew is normally written without vowels, but scripture and teaching texts add
niqqud — the points set above, below and inside the letters. They are combining
marks with no width of their own, so the engine reads the font's anchor data to
place each one on its consonant rather than letting it fall at the pen. Here is
the opening of Genesis, pointed:

\medskip

\rtl
{\font hebrew 16 regular
בְּרֵאשִׁית בָּרָא אֱלֹהִים אֵת הַשָּׁמַיִם וְאֵת הָאָרֶץ׃
}
\ltr

\section{Bidirectional text}

Latin words and numbers keep their own left-to-right order inside a right-to-left
line: the algorithm treats each as an embedded island and only the Hebrew around
them reverses. The line below names the translation in Latin script and gives the
chapter and verse range in digits, all within a Hebrew sentence.

\medskip

\rtl
{\font hebrew 14 regular
הבשורה על־פי יוחנן פרק 1 פסוקים 1 עד 5 בתרגום Delitzsch אל העברית׃
}
\ltr

\section{Hebrew embedded in English}

The reverse case needs no change of base direction. In an ordinary left-to-right
paragraph a Hebrew word — here {\font hebrew 12 regular דבר}, meaning ``word'' —
is reversed in place by the same algorithm, while the English around it is left
exactly as it was.

\section{The scholarly face}

A second Hebrew face is bundled: Ezra SIL, cut in the style of the Biblia Hebraica
Stuttgartensia and selected with \mono{\\font ezra}. It draws a letter and the point
inside it — a dagesh, a mapiq, a shin or sin dot — as a single shape rather than as a
letter with a mark laid over it, which is what a critical edition sets. The engine
combines the pair through the font's own composition feature, so the dagesh sits inside
its letter and not beside it:

\medskip

\rtl
{\font ezra 18 regular
בְּרֵאשִׁית בָּרָא אֱלֹהִים אֵת הַשָּׁמַיִם וְאֵת הָאָרֶץ׃
}
\ltr

\medskip

\rtl
{\font ezra 18 bold
וַיֹּאמֶר אֱלֹהִים יְהִי אוֹר וַיְהִי־אוֹר׃
}
\ltr
